{{Nayib Bukele sidebar}}

A crackdown on [[gangs]] began in [[El Salvador]] on 27 March 2022 in response to a series of [[Homicide|homicides]] committed by criminal gangs between 25 and 27 March 2022 which killed 87 people. After the killings, the Salvadoran government declared a [[state of emergency]] that suspended several constitutional rights and enabled the government to launch [[Mass arrest|mass arrests]] of suspected gang members. The crackdown and state of emergency has since been extended 53 times {{As of|2026|July|28|lc=y}}.<ref name="53 ext" /> In El Salvador, the crackdown has been called the "State of Exception" ({{langx|es|régimen de excepción}}) or the "War Against the Gangs" ({{lang|es|guerra contra las pandillas}}).

The State of Exception allowed authorities to make arrests without a warrant and gave the government access to citizens' communication. {{As of|2026|July|28}}, over 92,740 people accused of having gang affiliations have been arrested,<ref name="53 ext" /> which has overcrowded El Salvador's prisons and has led the country to have the highest [[incarceration rate]] in the world by 2023.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2023-09-12 |title=El Salvador Is Imprisoning People at Triple the Rate of the US |language=en |work=Bloomberg.com |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-09-12/el-salvador-jails-1-6-of-population-in-crime-crackdown |access-date=2023-10-30 }}</ref> As of 16 May 2023, 5,000 people who were arrested have been released.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2023-05-16 |title=Ministro de Seguridad confirma liberación de 5 mil detenidos durante el régimen de excepción |url=https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Ministro-de-Seguridad-confirma-liberacion-de-5-mil-detenidos-durante-el-regimen-de-excepcion-20230516-0017.html |access-date= |website=La Prensa Gráfica |language=Spanish |archive-date=21 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230521073227/https://www.laprensagrafica.com/elsalvador/Ministro-de-Seguridad-confirma-liberacion-de-5-mil-detenidos-durante-el-regimen-de-excepcion-20230516-0017.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Around two percent of El Salvador's adult population was incarcerated by early 2023.<ref name="auto">{{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/el-salvador-opens-40000-person-prison-arrests-soar-gang-crackdown-2023-02-01/|title=El Salvador Opens 40,000-Person Prison as Arrests Soar in Gang Crackdown|language=en|date=1 February 2023|access-date=2 February 2023|work=[[Reuters]]|first1=Nelson|last1=Renteria|first2=Sarah|last2=Kinosian|editor1-first=Sandra|editor1-last=Maler|archive-date=1 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230201220558/https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/el-salvador-opens-40000-person-prison-arrests-soar-gang-crackdown-2023-02-01/|url-status=live}}</ref> In January 2023, Minister of Defense [[René Merino Monroy]] announced that the government registered 496 homicides in 2022, a 56.8% decrease from 1,147 homicides in 2021. He attributed the decrease in homicides to the gang crackdown.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Renteria |first1=Nelson |date=3 January 2023 |title=El Salvador Murders Plummet by Over Half in 2022 Amid Gang Crackdown |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/el-salvador-murders-plummet-by-over-half-2022-amid-gang-crackdown-2023-01-03/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230103203737/https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/el-salvador-murders-plummet-by-over-half-2022-amid-gang-crackdown-2023-01-03/ |archive-date=3 January 2023 |access-date=5 January 2023 |work=[[Reuters]] |language=en |location=}}</ref> That same month, the government opened the [[Terrorism Confinement Center]] (CECOT), a prison with a capacity for 40,000 prisoners.<ref name="FT06">{{cite web|url=https://www.ft.com/content/d05a1b0a-f444-4337-99d2-84d9f0b59f95|title=Inside El Salvador's Mega Prison: The Jail Giving Inmates Less Space than Livestock|language=en|work=[[Financial Times]]|date=6 March 2023|access-date=8 March 2023|first1=Christine|last1=Murray|first2=Alan|last2=Smith|location=[[Mexico City]] and [[London]]|url-access=subscription|archive-date=7 March 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230307071545/https://www.ft.com/content/d05a1b0a-f444-4337-99d2-84d9f0b59f95|url-status=live}}</ref>

Domestically, the crackdown has been popular among [[Salvadorans]], many weary of gang violence. Conversely, international human rights groups expressed concern that the arrests were arbitrary and had little to do with gang violence, and several U.S. government representatives expressed concern about the violence in the country and the methods used to combat it; these comments were criticized by Salvadoran president [[Nayib Bukele]]. Politicians across Latin America — in countries such as Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica, Ecuador, Guatemala, Honduras, and Peru — have implemented or have called for the implementation of security policies similar to those implemented by Bukele.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/world-nation/story/2023-07-25/growing-cult-of-nayib-bukele|title=Inside the Growing Cult of El Salvador's Nayib Bukele, Latin America's Political Star|language=en|date=25 July 2023|access-date=15 August 2023|work=[[Los Angeles Times]]|first1=Kate|last1=Linthicum|location=[[Mexico City]], [[Mexico]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230725100859/https://www.latimes.com/world-nation/story/2023-07-25/growing-cult-of-nayib-bukele|archive-date=25 July 2023|url-status=live}}</ref> 

El Salvador's security policies under President Nayib Bukele have been credited with significantly reducing crime; however, efforts to replicate them in other countries have faced challenges. Analysts argue that the strategy's effectiveness in El Salvador was influenced by specific factors, including the country's geography, demographics, security force capabilities, incarceration rates, and political context. The application of similar measures in other nations has not yielded comparable results, as differing conditions may limit their effectiveness. Critics suggest that some governments adopting similar policies risk reinforcing ineffective heavy-handed security approaches that have historically struggled to address crime in a sustainable manner.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Kurylo |first=Benjamin |title=The flawed appeal of the Bukele method in the Americas |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14678802.2025.2464604 |journal=Conflict, Security & Development |date=2025 |issue=2 |volume=25 |pages=209–249 |doi=10.1080/14678802.2025.2464604 |issn=1467-8802|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

== Name ==

The gang crackdown is officially known in [[El Salvador]] as the "[[State of exception|State of Exception]]" ({{langx|es|régimen de excepción}}).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hrw.org/es/news/2022/03/29/el-salvador-amplio-regimen-de-excepcion-facilita-graves-abusos|title=El Salvador: Amplio "Régimen de Excepción" Facilita Graves Abusos|trans-title=El Salvador: Broad "State of Exception" Facilitates Serious Abuses|language=es|date=29 March 2022|access-date=6 October 2024|work=[[Human Rights Watch]]}}</ref> Salvadoran president [[Nayib Bukele]] and his government have described the crackdown itself as a "[[war]]" ({{lang|es|guerra}})<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.barrons.com/articles/la-guerra-de-bukele-contra-las-pandillas-inspira-un-videojuego-1da5b8f4|title=La "Guerra" de Bukele Contra las Pandillas Inspira un Videojuego|trans-title=Bukele's "War" Against the Gangs Inspires a Video Game|language=es|date=15 April 2023|access-date=6 October 2024|work=[[Barron's]]}}</ref> and also refer to it as the "War Against the Gangs" ({{lang|es|guerra contra las pandillas}}).<ref>{{cite web|last1=Cl |first1=Cooperativa |url=https://cooperativa.cl/noticias/mundo/america-latina/el-salvador/el-salvador-cumple-dos-anos-de-la-guerra-contra-las-pandillas/2024-03-27/204506.html|title=El Salvador Cumple Dos Años de la "Guerra Contra las Pandillas"|trans-title=El Salvador Completes Two Years of the "War Against the Gangs"|language=es|date=27 March 2024|access-date=6 October 2024|work=[[Radio Cooperativa]]}}</ref>

== Background ==
[[File:Gang presence in El Salvador, 2022.png|thumb|250px|Map of gang presence in El Salvador before the 2022 crackdown]]
=== Crime in El Salvador ===

For decades, most [[crime in El Salvador]] was committed by the country's two largest criminal gangs (also known as {{lang|es|[[Mara (gang)|maras]]}}): [[MS-13|Mara Salvatrucha]] (more commonly known as MS-13) and [[18th Street gang]] (Barrio 18).<ref name="Ribando">{{cite web|url=https://sgp.fas.org/crs/row/RS22141.pdf|title=Gangs in Central America|language=en|date=10 May 2005|access-date=7 October 2024|work=[[Congressional Research Service]]|first1=Clare|last1=Ribando|pages=1–6|archive-date=8 June 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230608111306/https://sgp.fas.org/crs/row/RS22141.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref>{{Rp|1}} MS-13 was created in [[Los Angeles]] by refugees who fled [[El Salvador]] in the 1980s due to the [[Salvadoran Civil War|civil war]] that was ongoing at the time; Barrio 18{{efn|Since 2004, Barrio 18 has been split into two rival [[clique]]s: Barrio 18 Revolucionarios and Barrio 18 Sureños.<ref name="ICG">{{cite web|url=https://www.crisisgroup.org/sites/default/files/064-el-salvador-s-politics-of-perpetual-violence.pdf|title=El Salvador's Politics of Perpetual Violence|language=en|date=19 December 2017|access-date=7 October 2024|work=[[International Crisis Group]]|location=[[Brussels]], Belgium}}</ref>{{Rp|8}}}} was created by Mexican immigrants in the 1960s who were not accepted into other Hispanic gangs, and it accepted Hispanics of all nationalities unlike other gangs at the time. These gangs arrived in El Salvador in the 1990s after the [[United States]] began deporting refugees back to the country after the civil war's conclusion in 1992. Poverty, a lack of economic opportunities, high urbanization rates, and a lack of government counter-gang measures led to many young Salvadorans joining gangs,<ref name="Ribando" />{{Rp|2}} while others were forced to join the gangs under threat of violence.<ref name="Guide">{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/world-news/general-news-cb40308c8ae5f102d4547eaf04637bbd|title=Guía Básica para Entender a las Pandillas de El Salvador|trans-title=A Basic Guide to Understand El Salvador's Gangs|language=es|date=20 June 2023|access-date=7 October 2024|work=[[Associated Press]]|first1=María Teresa|last1=Hernández|first2=Marcos|last2=Alemán|location=[[San Salvador]], El Salvador}}</ref>

MS-13 and Barrio 18 engage in several types of crime such as murder, rape, kidnapping, arms trafficking, drug trafficking, intimidation, robbery, vehicular theft, and [[extortion]].<ref name="Hume">{{cite journal|first1=Mo|last1=Hume|title=Mano Dura: El Salvador Responds to Gangs|language=en|date=November 2007|journal=Development in Practice|publisher=Taylor & Francis, Ltd.|volume=17|issue=6|pages=739–751|doi=10.1080/09614520701628121|issn=0961-4524|jstor=25548280|oclc=9974001867|s2cid=144984287}}</ref>{{Rp|742}}<ref>{{cite web|url=http://sustainablesecurity.org/article/mano-dura-gang-suppression-el-salvador|title=Mano Dura: Gang Suppression in El Salvador|language=en|date=March 2011|access-date=7 October 2024|work=Sustainable Security|first1=Sonja|last1=Wolf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130511183052/http://sustainablesecurity.org/article/mano-dura-gang-suppression-el-salvador|archive-date=11 May 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref> Both gangs divided El Salvador into territories, and by 2016, they had a presence in 247 of El Salvador's [[Municipalities of El Salvador|262 municipalities]].<ref name="ICG" />{{Rp|9}} The gangs charged [[extortion]] fees to people trying to enter or leave their controlled territories. Businesses were also forced to pay these extortion fees. The gangs intimidated people to not report crimes to the police with the [[unwritten rule]] of "see, hear, and be quiet" ("{{lang|es|ver, oír y callar}}") under threat of violence, often through murdering or raping relatives.<ref name="Guide" /> Gang violence contributed to El Salvador having one of the [[List of countries by intentional homicide rate|highest homicide rates in the world]] for decades;<ref name="ICG" />{{Rp|9}} in 2015, El Salvador's homicide rate reached 103 homicides per 100,000 people — of 6,650 homicides registered that year — making it the most violent country in the [[Western Hemisphere]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://insightcrime.org/news/analysis/insight-crime-homicide-round-up-2015-latin-america-caribbean/|title=InSight Crime's 2015 Homicide Round-Up|language=en|date=14 January 2016|access-date=22 August 2022|work=[[InSight Crime]]|first1=David|last1=Gagne}}</ref><ref name="YECR">{{cite journal|first1=Yovani E.|last1=Chavez Rodriguez|url=https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/jss/vol17/iss1/6/|title=Bukele's Formula for Terrorism|language=en|date=2024|journal=[[Journal of Strategic Security]]|publisher=[[University of South Florida]] Global and National Strategic Security Institute|volume=17|issue=1|pages=76–99|doi=10.5038/1944-0472.17.1.2186|issn=1944-0464|jstor=48766108|jstor-access=free|oclc=10298565763|access-date=20 November 2024|doi-access=free}}</ref>{{Rp|78}} That year, the [[Supreme Court of Justice of El Salvador|Supreme Court of Justice]] designated both MS-13 and Barrio 18 as [[List of designated terrorist groups|terrorist organizations]].<ref name="YECR" />{{Rp|93}}

In 2004, there were between 10,000 and 39,000 gang members in El Salvador;<ref name="Hume" />{{Rp|741}} in 2012, there were around 60,000 gang members.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://insightcrime.org/news/brief/el-salvador-sees-almost-60-drop-in-murders-in-april-as-gang-truce-holds/|title=El Salvador Sees Almost 60% Drop in Murders in April, As Gang Truce Holds|language=en|date=1 May 2012|access-date=7 October 2024|work=[[InSight Crime]]|first1=Hannah|last1=Stone}}</ref> According to the Salvadoran government, there were around 80,000 gang members by April 2022.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://elfaro.net/es/202206/columnas/26226/Las-viejas-recetas-de-Bukele-contra-las-pandillas.htm|title=Las Viejas Recetas de Bukele Contra las Pandillas|trans-title=Bukele's Old Recipes Against the Gangs|language=en|date=15 June 2022|access-date=7 October 2024|work=[[El Faro (digital newspaper)|El Faro]]|first1=Juan|last1=Pappier}}</ref> According to the Police Information System (SIP), a database utilized by the [[National Civil Police (El Salvador)|National Civil Police]] (PNC) to monitor gang activity, there were an estimated 120,000 gang members in El Salvador by August 2023. The SIP divides gang members into three categories: "homeboys" (full members), "{{lang|es|chequeos}}" (aspiring members), and collaborators, with most members being homeboys or collaborators.<ref name="SIP" />

=== Anti-gang policies ===

In June 2019, newly inaugurated President [[Nayib Bukele]] made combatting gang violence a priority for his administration,<ref name="FT1">{{cite news|last=Murray|first=Christine|date=5 April 2022|title=El Salvador's Gang Crackdown Prompts Fears of Growing Authoritarianism|url=https://www.ft.com/content/e71cdf99-fd37-474d-af49-c917e650a4e7|work=[[Financial Times]]|location=[[Mexico City]]|publication-place=[[London]]|language=en|access-date=6 April 2022|url-access=subscription|archive-date=6 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220406054316/https://www.ft.com/content/e71cdf99-fd37-474d-af49-c917e650a4e7|url-status=live}}</ref> and announced his "[[Territorial Control Plan]]" which sought to crack down on gang violence and improve security within the country. Actions taken by the country's security forces included the increase of military and police presence in specific [[Municipalities of El Salvador|municipalities]] where gang influence was significant, instituting lockdowns in prisons by confining prisoners to their cells and severing cell phone service in prisons, and improving the equipment and technology used by the [[National Civil Police (El Salvador)|National Civil Police]] (PNC) and the [[Salvadoran Army]].<ref name="AS">{{cite web|url=https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-nayib-bukeles-territorial-control-plan|title=Explainer: Nayib Bukele's Territorial Control Plan|date=13 February 2020|access-date=22 August 2022|work=[[Americas Society]]|language=en|first=Paola|last=Nagovitch|archive-date=23 August 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220823021814/https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-nayib-bukeles-territorial-control-plan|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2021, the homicide rate reached the lowest it has been since the [[Salvadoran Civil War]] ended in 1992, with 18 homicides per 100,000 people.<ref name="AJ">{{cite web|date=5 April 2022|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/5/un-rights-office-raises-concerns-over-el-salvador-gang-crackdown|title=UN Rights Office Raises Concerns About El Salvador Gang Crackdown|work=[[Al Jazeera Media Network|Al Jazeera]]|language=en|access-date=6 April 2022|archive-date=5 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220405195222/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/5/un-rights-office-raises-concerns-over-el-salvador-gang-crackdown|url-status=live}}</ref>  Bukele attributed this decline to his policies,<ref name="FT1" /> and it has been one of his most-touted accomplishments.<ref name="AP">{{cite news |last=Alemán |first=Marcos |date=29 March 2022 |title=El Salvador forces encircle neighborhoods in gang crackdown |work=Associated Press |location=San Salvador |url=https://apnews.com/article/business-caribbean-nayib-bukele-el-salvador-san-salvador-f19b74c8380888a735364b6622e6ba5f |access-date=6 April 2022 |archive-date=6 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220406193146/https://apnews.com/article/business-caribbean-nayib-bukele-el-salvador-san-salvador-f19b74c8380888a735364b6622e6ba5f |url-status=live }}</ref> Bukele enjoys an [[Opinion polling on the Nayib Bukele presidency|extremely high approval rating]] of approximately 85%,<ref name="AP" /> one of the highest in the world.<ref name="NYT: explosion">{{cite news|last1=Abi-Habib|first1=Maria|last2=Avelar|first2=Bryan|date=27 March 2022|title=Explosion of Gang Violence Grips El Salvador, Setting Record|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/27/world/americas/el-salvador-gang-violence.html|work=[[The New York Times]]|access-date=28 May 2022|archive-date=4 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220404000028/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/27/world/americas/el-salvador-gang-violence.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In December 2021, the United States government accused Bukele of negotiating a secret agreement with the gangs, reducing violence in return for financial and prison benefits.<ref name="Guardian: negotiations">{{cite news|date=8 December 2021|title=US accuses El Salvador of secretly negotiating truce with gang leaders|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/08/el-salvador-us-gang-leaders-truce|work=[[The Guardian]]|access-date=27 December 2023|archive-date=27 December 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231227111615/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/08/el-salvador-us-gang-leaders-truce|url-status=live}}</ref> Additionally, the [[United States Department of the Treasury]] sanctioned two Salvadoran officials it claimed conducted the talks.<ref name="El Faro: negotiations">{{cite news|date=28 February 2023|title=El Salvador Government Protection of MS-13 from Extradition Emerges in DOJ Indictment|url=https://elfaro.net/en/202302/el_salvador/26739/el-salvador-government-protection-of-ms-13-from-extradition-emerges-in-doj-indictment|work=[[El Faro (digital newspaper)|El Faro]]|access-date=27 December 2023|archive-date=27 December 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231227111615/https://elfaro.net/en/202302/el_salvador/26739/el-salvador-government-protection-of-ms-13-from-extradition-emerges-in-doj-indictment|url-status=live}}</ref> Bukele called the accusations of making a deal a "lie"<ref name="FT1"/> and denounced previous administrations who made similar deals.<ref name="AP" />

Between 24 and 27 April 2020, a total of 77 people were murdered in El Salvador. Bukele's government says that the spike in murders was organized by gang members within El Salvador's prisons, leading to him instituting lockdowns across the country's prisons. Prisoners were confined in their cells 24 hours per day and rival gang members were mixed together in cells.<ref name="HRW">{{cite web|url=https://www.hrw.org/news/2020/04/29/el-salvador-inhumane-prison-lockdown-treatment|title=El Salvador: Inhumane Prison Lockdown Treatment|language=en|date=29 April 2020|access-date=23 August 2022|work=[[Human Rights Watch]]|archive-date=29 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200429231346/https://www.hrw.org/news/2020/04/29/el-salvador-inhumane-prison-lockdown-treatment|url-status=live}}</ref> His government published photographs of inmates being rounded up on the prison floor in cramped conditions.<ref name="WSP">{{cite news |last1=Sheridan |first1=Mary Beth |last2=Brigida |first2=Anna-Catherine |date=April 27, 2020 |title=Photos Show El Salvador's Crackdown on Imprisoned Gang Members |language=en |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/the_americas/el-salvador-prison-crackdown-nayib-bukele/2020/04/27/5d3cea4c-88c9-11ea-80df-d24b35a568ae_story.html |url-status=live |url-access=subscription |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200428041816/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/the_americas/el-salvador-prison-crackdown-nayib-bukele/2020/04/27/5d3cea4c-88c9-11ea-80df-d24b35a568ae_story.html |archive-date=28 April 2020}}</ref> [[Human Rights Watch]] (HRW) criticized the government's actions as a [[Human rights violation|violation of human rights]] and that it gave no consideration to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in El Salvador|COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref name="HRW" />

=== March 2022 homicide spike ===

From 25 to 27 March 2022, 87 people were murdered in El Salvador,<ref name="Guardian: lockdown">{{cite news|date=28 March 2022|title=El Salvador locks down prisons after wave of 87 killings over weekend|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/mar/28/el-salvador-prisons-gang-killings|work=The Guardian|agency=Associated Press|access-date=6 April 2022|archive-date=5 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220405203933/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/mar/28/el-salvador-prisons-gang-killings|url-status=live}}</ref> including 62 people on Saturday alone,<ref name="FT1"/> the highest single-day tally in decades;<ref name="AJ"/> by contrast, 79 people were murdered throughout the entire month of February.<ref name="Guardian: lockdown"/> The victims were targeted randomly.<ref name="NYTimes">{{cite news|last1=Abi-Habib|first1=Maria|last2=Avelar|first2=Bryan|date=28 March 2022|title=Hundreds Arrested in El Salvador, Raising Fears of a Civil Liberties Crackdown|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/28/world/americas/el-salvador-gang-violence-bukele.html|work=The New York Times|access-date=6 April 2022|archive-date=6 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220406081952/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/28/world/americas/el-salvador-gang-violence-bukele.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The government blamed the violence on [[Mara Salvatrucha]] (MS-13).<ref name="Guardian: 6k arrested">{{cite news|last1=Avelar|first1=Bryan|last2=Lakhani|first2=Nina|date=5 April 2022|title=El Salvador reels as 6,000 people arrested in unprecedented crackdown|url=https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2022/apr/05/el-salvador-crackdown-ms13-state-of-emergency|work=The Guardian|location=San Salvador and Santa Tecla|publication-place=London|access-date=6 April 2022|archive-date=5 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220405233621/https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2022/apr/05/el-salvador-crackdown-ms13-state-of-emergency|url-status=live}}</ref> William Eulises Soriano Herrera, a member of Bukele's [[Nuevas Ideas]] party, suggested the spike in violence was retaliation for the government's seizing control of two bus routes in the capital, which gangs often extort for revenue;<ref name="AP" /> according to José Miguel Cruz, a research director at [[Florida International University]], the gangs may have been sending a message to the government to try to obtain better terms.<ref name="FT1"/>

== Government crackdown ==

[[File:Legislative Assembly of El Salvador voting to implement the State of Exception.png|thumb|right|The Legislative Assembly voting to implement the state of exception]]
[[File:National Civil Police patrol in Zaragoza, El Salvador (July 2022).jpg|thumb|right|A police patrol in [[Zaragoza, La Libertad|Zaragoza]] during the crackdown]]
[[File:Salvadoran Army patrol in Santa Tecla, El Salvador (July 2022).jpg|thumb|right|An army patrol in [[Santa Tecla, El Salvador|Santa Tecla]] during the crackdown]]

During an extraordinary session early on 27 March 2022, the [[Legislative Assembly of El Salvador|Legislative Assembly]] approved a "state of exception".<ref name="AJ: state of emergency">{{cite news|date=28 March 2022|title=El Salvador declares state of emergency after gang killings|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/3/28/el-salvador-invokes-state-of-emergency-after-62-killings-in-a-day|work=Al Jazeera|access-date=6 April 2022|archive-date=5 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220405234029/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/3/28/el-salvador-invokes-state-of-emergency-after-62-killings-in-a-day|url-status=live}}</ref> The order, which was initially set to last thirty days but has been extended 53 times,<ref name="53 ext" /> suspended the rights of [[right of association|association]] and [[right to counsel|legal counsel]], increased the amount of time that persons may be detained without being [[criminal charge|charged]] from three days to fifteen, and permitted the government to monitor citizens' communications without [[search warrant|warrants]].<ref name="AJ: state of emergency"/> However, Salvadoran law enforcement was accused of violating even the expanded limits of their powers.<ref name="AP" /><ref name="Guardian: 6k arrested"/> The government also restricted the ability of judges to offer prisoners alternatives to pre-trial detention, like bail and house arrest.<ref name="AJ: pressure">{{cite news|last=Brigida|first=Anna-Cat|date=26 May 2022|title=Pressure to make arrests as El Salvador extends gang crackdown|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/5/26/pressure-to-make-arrests-as-el-salvador-extends-gang-crackdown|work=Al Jazeera|access-date=28 May 2022|archive-date=27 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220527134844/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/5/26/pressure-to-make-arrests-as-el-salvador-extends-gang-crackdown|url-status=live}}</ref>

Members of Nuevas Ideas passed new rules that increase prison sentences for convicted gang lords to forty to forty-five years (previously six to nine) and twenty to thirty years for other members (previously three to five)<ref name="AJ"/> and reduce the [[age of criminal responsibility]], previously sixteen years old,<ref name="AJ: pressure"/> to twelve.<ref name="AP: extension">{{cite news|date=25 May 2022|title=El Salvador extends anti-gang emergency for another month|url=https://apnews.com/article/caribbean-nayib-bukele-el-salvador-san-gangs-ba0171149f83f6099d0bebde8f5a3d2d|work=Associated Press|location=San Salvador|publication-place=New York City|access-date=28 May 2022|archive-date=28 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220528170843/https://apnews.com/article/caribbean-nayib-bukele-el-salvador-san-gangs-ba0171149f83f6099d0bebde8f5a3d2d|url-status=live}}</ref> They also passed a law that threatens anyone who reproduces or disseminates messages from gangs, including news media, with ten to fifteen years in prison.<ref name="AJ: extension 1">{{cite news|date=25 April 2022|title=El Salvador extends state of emergency to curb gang violence|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/25/el-salvador-extends-state-of-emergency-gang-violence-crackdown|work=Al Jazeera|access-date=28 May 2022|archive-date=25 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220425132420/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/25/el-salvador-extends-state-of-emergency-gang-violence-crackdown|url-status=live}}</ref>

The government deployed additional police and military forces,<ref name="AJ: surge">{{cite news|last=Brigida|first=Anna-Cat|date=28 March 2022|title=Surge in gang killings spurs fear, uncertainty in El Salvador|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/3/28/surge-in-gang-killings-spurs-fear-uncertainty-in-el-salvador|work=Al Jazeera|access-date=6 April 2022|archive-date=5 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220405234036/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/3/28/surge-in-gang-killings-spurs-fear-uncertainty-in-el-salvador|url-status=live}}</ref> raiding houses and creating checkpoints surrounding neighborhoods with known gang presences.<ref name="FT1"/> Soldiers checked everyone for identification cards and proof of address,<ref name="Guardian: 6k arrested"/> searched their vehicles and backpacks,<ref name="AP" /> and refused to let anyone enter or leave without what they considered a legitimate reason.<ref name="Guardian: 6k arrested"/> Anyone considered suspicious was forced to strip so soldiers could check for gang-related tattoos.<ref name="Guardian: 6k arrested"/>

By the end of 27 March, Salvadoran security forces claimed to have detained 576 people;<ref name="NYTimes"/> within a week, almost 6,000 people had been arrested, straining El Salvador's already-overcrowded prisons.<ref name="Guardian: 6k arrested"/> Nevertheless, the government pressured the police, military, and judicial system to continue the mass arrests by setting arrest quotas.<ref name="AJ: pressure"/> After one month, more than 17,000 people had been arrested;<ref name="AJ: extension 1"/> on 25 May, the [[National Civil Police (El Salvador)|National Civil Police]] announced that more than 34,500 people had been arrested since the start of the state of emergency.<ref name="AJ: pressure"/> Many people were unable to learn where or why relatives were detained.<ref name="NYTimes"/> Rations for prisoners were reduced to two meals each day<ref name="Guardian: lockdown"/> consisting of only beans and tortillas;<ref name="FT1"/> Bukele explained that he would "not take budget away from schools to feed these terrorists".<ref name="AJ: state of emergency"/> Prisoners have also been denied mattresses and [[frog march]]ed,<ref name="AP: extension"/> and Bukele ordered that all gang members be confined to their cells 24/7.<ref name="AJ: state of emergency"/>

In July 2022, Bukele announced the construction of a new prison which will be able to house 40,000 prisoners, making it one of the largest prisons in the world.<ref name="FT2">{{cite news|last=Agren|first=David|date=23 August 2022|title=El Salvador Builds 40,000-Inmate Mega-Prison in "War Against Gangs"|url=https://www.ft.com/content/96eca255-1346-4a90-be0b-738361b1b317|work=[[Financial Times]]|location=[[Mexico City]]|publication-place=[[London]]|language=en|access-date=23 August 2022|url-access=subscription|archive-date=10 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230110210448/https://www.ft.com/content/96eca255-1346-4a90-be0b-738361b1b317|url-status=live}}</ref> The prison, known as the [[Terrorism Confinement Center]] and located in [[Tecoluca]], opened on 31 January 2023.<ref name="auto"/>
[[File:Elementos de seguridad en CECOT.jpg|thumb|right|240px|Military and police staff of the [[Terrorism Confinement Center]]]]
On 3 November 2022, [[Osiris Luna|Osiris Luna Meza]], the minister of justice, announced that the government would begin destroying [[gravestone]]s belonging to members of gangs to prevent them from becoming "shrines", stating "terrorists will no longer be able to 'glorify' the memory of dead criminals". Despite the destruction of the gravestones, the government stated that the bodies would remain intact and not be disturbed.<ref name="graves">{{cite web|url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20221103-el-salvador-destroys-gang-members-gravestones|title=El Salvador Destroys Gang Members' Gravestones|language=en|date=3 November 2022|access-date=3 November 2022|work=[[France 24]]|archive-date=3 November 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221103215247/http://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20221103-el-salvador-destroys-gang-members-gravestones|url-status=live}}</ref> Bukele compared the removal of the tombstones of gang members to [[denazification]] in [[Allied-occupied Germany]] and also compared the gangs to the [[Nazism|Nazis]] themselves. He also stated that the government policy is only aimed at gravestones that mention gang affiliation.<ref>{{Cite tweet|last1=Bukele|first1=Nayib|author-link=Nayib Bukele|user=nayibbukele|number=1590864386905628672|date=10 November 2022|title=¿Por qué destruimos las lápidas en las tumbas de los pandilleros?|trans-title=Why do we destroy the headstones of the tombs of gang members?|language=es|access-date=10 November 2022}}</ref>

The government initiated a [[Siege of Soyapango|siege]] of the city of [[Soyapango]] on 3 December 2022 when 10,000 soldiers were sent to surround the city and arrest gang members.<ref name="BBC">{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-63846159|title=El Salvador: Thousands of Troops Surround City in Gang Crackdown|date=4 December 2022|access-date=20 December 2022|language=en|work=[[BBC]]|first1=Matt|last1=Murphy|archive-date=12 December 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221212140032/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-63846159|url-status=live}}</ref> The government erased gang graffiti from the city during the siege.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://diariolahuella.com/eliminan-grafitis-en-el-municipio-de-soyapango/|title=Eliminan grafitis en el municipio de Soyapango|date=25 January 2023|access-date=26 January 2023|archive-date=26 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230126050452/https://diariolahuella.com/eliminan-grafitis-en-el-municipio-de-soyapango/|url-status=live}}</ref>

{{As of|2026|July|28}}, the state of exception has been extended 53 times by the Legislative Assembly.<ref name="53 ext" /> [[Nuevas Ideas]] (NI), the [[Grand Alliance for National Unity]] (GANA), the [[Christian Democratic Party (El Salvador)|Christian Democratic Party]] (PDC), the [[National Coalition Party (El Salvador)|National Coalition Party]] (PCN), and three [[independent politician]]s consistently vote for the extensions, while the [[Nationalist Republican Alliance]] (ARENA), the [[Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front]] (FMLN), [[Nuestro Tiempo (El Salvador)|Nuestro Tiempo]] (NT), and [[Vamos (El Salvador)|Vamos]] (V) consistently vote against it or abstain.{{citation needed|date=October 2024}} In March 2026, the Legislative Assembly approved a constitutional amendment to allow the imposition of [[life imprisonment]] upon individuals convicted on murder, rape, or terrorism.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/el-salvador-reforms-constitution-allow-life-sentences-2026-03-17/|title=El Salvador Reforms Constitution to Allow for Life Sentences|language=en|date=17 March 2026|access-date=17 March 2026|work=[[Reuters]]|editor1-first=Natalia|editor1-last=Siniawski}}</ref>

== Reactions ==
=== Domestic response===
Domestically, the crackdown is popular.<ref name="Guardian: 6k arrested"/> An April 2022 Gallup poll found that 91% of Salvadorans supported the government's actions, including 78% who "very much" supported them.<ref>{{cite report|date=April 2022|title=Población de acuerdo con medidas tomadas por el gobierno contra pandilleros|url=https://www.cidgallup.com/uploads/publication/publication_files/publication_1650568201000.pdf|publisher=CID Gallup|language=es|access-date=28 May 2022|archive-date=30 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530205127/https://www.cidgallup.com/uploads/publication/publication_files/publication_1650568201000.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> Many Salvadorans explained that they were weary of violence,<ref name="AJ: extension 2">{{cite news|date=26 May 2022|title=El Salvador extends state of emergency amid gang crackdown|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/5/26/el-salvador-extends-state-of-emergency-amid-gang-crackdown|work=Al Jazeera|access-date=28 May 2022|archive-date=28 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220528170911/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/5/26/el-salvador-extends-state-of-emergency-amid-gang-crackdown|url-status=live}}</ref> and many Salvadorans living in the United States supported the crackdown.<ref name="NBC">{{cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/latino/many-salvadorans-us-support-countrys-president-massive-crackdown-abuse-rcna36270|title=Many Salvadorans in U.S. Support Country's President Amid Massive Crackdown, Abuse Allegations|language=en|work=[[NBC News]]|date=29 July 2022|access-date=31 July 2022|first1=Carmen|last1=Sesin|first2=Edwin|last2=Flores|archive-date=31 July 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220731030230/https://www.nbcnews.com/news/latino/many-salvadorans-us-support-countrys-president-massive-crackdown-abuse-rcna36270|url-status=live}}</ref> Archbishop of San Salvador [[José Luis Escobar Alas]] voiced his open support for the crackdown in July 2022.<ref name="AP arch">{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/religion-el-salvador-san-gangs-e82cc0c848d632a0dfc9dd4a95b7c3e2|title=Salvadoran Archbishop Praises Government Crackdown on Gangs|language=en|work=[[Associated Press]]|date=31 July 2022|access-date=23 August 2022|archive-date=23 August 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220823233533/https://apnews.com/article/religion-el-salvador-san-gangs-e82cc0c848d632a0dfc9dd4a95b7c3e2|url-status=live}}</ref>

In general, the people living in El Salvador consider the atmosphere in the neighborhoods to be much safer, with citizens and observers saying they feel far more free to do what would have been previously considered unthinkable, with journalist Ioan Grillo writing "I have interviewed dozens of gang members in visits over the years; you could often find them openly hanging out in the centre of neighbourhoods. But since the state of emergency, their presence has been greatly reduced: Maras are largely in prison, have fled, or are underground". Similarly, Mary Speck has observed that the criminal gangs have "largely disappeared" from [[San Salvador]] because of which vendors, shop owners, drivers and pedestrians no longer have to pay gang members and that residents of a particular block dominated by a gang "no longer fear violent reprisals if they cross into another gang’s territory".<ref name="Ach">{{Cite web |title=As El Salvador's Gang Crackdown Continues, Citizens Wonder What's Next? |url=https://www.usip.org/publications/2023/05/el-salvadors-gang-crackdown-continues-citizens-wonder-whats-next |work=[[United States Institute of Peace]] |access-date=16 May 2023 |archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516115806/https://www.usip.org/publications/2023/05/el-salvadors-gang-crackdown-continues-citizens-wonder-whats-next |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="jaja">{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/09/world/americas/el-salvador-gangs.html#:~:text=But%20that%20achievement%2C%20critics%20say,willing%20to%20accept%20that%20deal. |title=El Salvador Decimated It's ruthless gangs. But at what cost? |work=[[New York Times]] |date=9 April 2023 |last1=Kitroeff |first1=Natalie |last2=Volpe |first2=Daniele |access-date=16 May 2023 |archive-date=13 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230413133030/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/09/world/americas/el-salvador-gangs.html#:~:text=But%20that%20achievement%2C%20critics%20say,willing%20to%20accept%20that%20deal. |url-status=live }}</ref> However, the incidents of innocent people getting caught up in the crackdown have remained a significant concern about the war against the gangs, with the families of some of those arrested in the crackdown saying that their family members were arrested on the basis of poor evidence and an advocacy group for such people called "Victims of the Regime Movement" being founded.<ref name="twi">{{cite web |date=31 July 2022 |title=MOVIRSV |url=https://twitter.com/MOVIRSV |access-date= |website=twitter. com |archive-date=7 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230307192214/https://twitter.com/movirsv |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Blitzer |first=Jonathan |date=2022-09-05 |title=The Rise of Nayib Bukele, El Salvador's Authoritarian President |language=en-US |magazine=The New Yorker |url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2022/09/12/the-rise-of-nayib-bukele-el-salvadors-authoritarian-president |access-date=2023-05-16 |issn=0028-792X |archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516123528/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2022/09/12/the-rise-of-nayib-bukele-el-salvadors-authoritarian-president |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Irreconocible: liberan a lanchero tras un año detenido por el régimen de excepción |url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/regimen-de-excepcion-puerto-el-triunfo-/1060037/2023/ |access-date=2023-05-16 |website=Noticias de El Salvador - Noticias de El Salvador, noticias internacionales, salvadoreños por el mundo, economia, negocios, politica, deportes, entretenimiento, tecnologia, turismo, tendencias, fotos, videos, redes sociales |language=en |archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516123352/https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/regimen-de-excepcion-puerto-el-triunfo-/1060037/2023/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===United States response===
The [[United States Institute of Peace]] observed that while the control asserted by the gangs in El Salvador had indeed been greatly diminished, if not destroyed, the government would still need to formulate a plan for transitioning out of the state of exception and figure out what to do with those arrested. It also mentioned the need for the government to step in and use the newly regained control over the gangs to prevent more people from becoming gang members and replacing the present gang members who have been sent to jail. Veronica Reyna, a researcher from El Salvador, was quoted as saying, “The gangs exercised control because no one else would. If the state doesn’t fill this vacuum, not just with police but education and other services, other criminal groups will step in."{{Citation needed|date=July 2025|reason=Direct quote}} The state of exception is expected to continue until the March 2024 general elections, after which anti-crime measures addressing causes like education and underdevelopment are expected. An unnamed security expert was quoted as saying, “They know they can’t simply fill up the jails. After the elections, they can start looking at rehabilitation and community engagement."{{Citation needed|date=July 2025|reason=Direct quote}} 

So far,{{When|date=July 2025|reason=ambiguous}} the government has promised to deal with these issues with initiatives such as Mi Nueva Escuela (My New School), which aims to improve the educational situation through curriculum reform, teacher training, arrangement of quality didactic, technological, health, and nutritional facilities, as well as the remodeling of more than 5,000 schools over the next five years. Additionally, CUBOs (Urban Centers) aims to provide impoverished youths with safe spaces and has already been launched. One thousand schools are expected to be rebuilt by September 2023 under Mi Nueva Escuela, while 11 transparent, cube-shaped urban centers in poor communities, offering young people academic support, athletic activities, and art or language classes with access to computers, a library with cozy cushions for reading, and adult supervision have already been built as of May 2023.{{Update inline|date=October 2023}} While these initiatives have been appreciated, activists feel that more work is required as children in some areas still lack access to schools.<ref name="Ach" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-09-08 |title=Presidente Nayib Bukele lanza reforma educativa "Mi Nueva Escuela", para transformar la educación desde la formación docente hasta la infraestructura escolar |url=https://www.presidencia.gob.sv/presidente-nayib-bukele-lanza-reforma-educativa-mi-nueva-escuela-para-transformar-la-educacion-desde-la-formacion-docente-hasta-la-infraestructura-escolar/ |access-date=2023-05-17 |website=Presidencia de la República de El Salvador |language=es |archive-date=17 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230517110638/https://www.presidencia.gob.sv/presidente-nayib-bukele-lanza-reforma-educativa-mi-nueva-escuela-para-transformar-la-educacion-desde-la-formacion-docente-hasta-la-infraestructura-escolar/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Conservatives, including those belonging to U.S. [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]] circles have also talked about the crackdown in a positive manner.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Beauchamp |first=Zack |date=2023-04-08 |title=Meet the MAGA movement's new favorite autocrat |url=https://www.vox.com/2023/4/8/23653324/nayib-bukele-trump-american-right-crime |access-date=2023-06-13 |website=Vox |language=en |archive-date=8 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230408120320/https://www.vox.com/2023/4/8/23653324/nayib-bukele-trump-american-right-crime |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-03-10 |title=Nayib Bukele Is No Conservative Hero |url=https://www.nationalreview.com/2023/03/nayib-bukele-is-no-conservative-hero/ |access-date=2023-06-13 |website=National Review |language=en-US |archive-date=20 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230520054615/https://www.nationalreview.com/2023/03/nayib-bukele-is-no-conservative-hero/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In a tweet on 10 April 2022, U.S. Secretary of State [[Antony Blinken]] "condemn[ed]" the increase in gang violence in El Salvador and "urge[d]" the government to respect due process and civil liberties.<ref>{{cite tweet|last=Blinken|first=Antony|user=SecBlinken|number=1513143018571894793|date=10 April 2022|title=We condemn the increased gang violence and homicides in El Salvador. We also urge the Government of El Salvador to uphold due process and protect civil liberties, including freedoms of press, peaceful assembly, and expression..|access-date=28 May 2022}}</ref> The same day, State Department spokesman [[Ned Price]] tweeted affirming that the United States "continues to support El Salvador" against gangs while urging it to protect its citizens and their civil liberties.<ref>{{cite tweet|last=Price|first=Ned|user=StateDeptSpox|number=1513145186964103172|date=10 April 2022|title=The U.S. government continues to support El Salvador in its efforts to reduce the proliferation of gangs. We urge El Salvador to protect its citizens while also upholding civil liberties, including freedom of the press..|access-date=28 May 2022}}</ref> The next day, Bukele responded that United States' support against Salvadoran gangs had come under the [[First presidency of Donald Trump|Trump administration]], citing the withdrawal of American aid from the National Civil Police and Institute for Access to Public Information four months into the [[Presidency of Joe Biden|Biden administration]], and saying that the United States now only supported the civil liberties of gangs.<ref name="EF"/>

===Latin American response===
In Latin America, the crackdown has attracted the attention of leaders and people from many countries with prominent Guatemalan presidential candidates [[Zury Ríos]] and [[Sandra Torres]], Honduran Security Minister Ramón Sibbilón, and Costa Rican security minister Jorge Torres applauding it. In Peru, the [[Mayor of Lima]] and right-wing presidential hopeful [[Rafael López Aliaga]] has praised the policies and called on the [[Ministry of Defence (Peru)|Peruvian Defense Ministry]] to allow the [[Peruvian Armed Forces]] to patrol the streets of Lima, including with [[tanks]] while [[Rodolfo Hernández Suárez]], who narrowly lost the [[2022 Colombian presidential election]] had promised similar policies. Honduras, under the leadership of left wing president [[Xiomara Castro]], has also launched its own, smaller crackdown, the [[Honduran gang crackdown]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=El Salvador's authoritarian president is becoming a regional role model |newspaper=The Economist |url=https://www.economist.com/the-americas/2023/03/16/el-salvadors-authoritarian-president-is-becoming-a-regional-role-model |access-date=2023-06-13 |issn=0013-0613 |archive-date=20 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230320051658/https://www.economist.com/the-americas/2023/03/16/el-salvadors-authoritarian-president-is-becoming-a-regional-role-model |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="ttys">{{Cite web |last=Bosworth |first=James |date=2023-03-06 |title=Bukele's 'Cool Authoritarian' Model Makes for a Dangerous Export |url=https://www.worldpoliticsreview.com/bukele-el-salvador-gangs-human-rights-ms-13/ |access-date=2023-06-13 |website=World Politics Review |language=en-US |archive-date=13 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230613072633/https://www.worldpoliticsreview.com/bukele-el-salvador-gangs-human-rights-ms-13/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Delta">{{Cite web |date=February 16, 2023 |first=Will |last=Freeman |title=Nayib Bukele's Growing List of Latin American Admirers |url=https://americasquarterly.org/article/nayib-bukeles-growing-list-of-latin-american-admirers/ |access-date=2023-06-13 |website=Americas Quarterly |language=en-US |archive-date=2 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602193949/https://americasquarterly.org/article/nayib-bukeles-growing-list-of-latin-american-admirers/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Honduras later temporarily intensified its crackdown in June 2023 with a prison sweep after a gang massacre inside a prison.<ref>{{Cite news |agency=Associated Press |date=2023-06-27 |title=Honduras starts El Salvador-style crackdown on gangs after massacres |language=en-GB |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/26/honduras-gang-violence-crackdown-pool-hall-shooting |access-date=2023-08-01 |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=1 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230801104411/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jun/26/honduras-gang-violence-crackdown-pool-hall-shooting |url-status=live }}</ref> Former Mexican president [[Andrés Manuel López Obrador]] and Ecuadorian President  [[Guillermo Lasso]]  have also expressed their views{{which|date=May 2026}} about the crackdown.<ref name="Delta" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Maldonado |first=Carlos S. |date=2023-05-29 |title=López Obrador critica la política de mano dura en seguridad de Bukele en El Salvador: "Hay autoritarismo y no respetan las libertades" |url=https://elpais.com/mexico/2023-05-29/lopez-obrador-critica-la-politica-de-mano-dura-en-seguridad-de-bukele-en-el-salvador-hay-autoritarismo-y-no-respetan-las-libertades.html |access-date=2023-08-01 |website=El País México |language=es-MX |archive-date=1 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230801103724/https://elpais.com/mexico/2023-05-29/lopez-obrador-critica-la-politica-de-mano-dura-en-seguridad-de-bukele-en-el-salvador-hay-autoritarismo-y-no-respetan-las-libertades.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[2023 Ecuadorian general election]] candidate [[Jan Topić|Jan Topíc]] has also styled himself as a Bukele-like political figure in his campaign aiming to fight crime with a hard hand, earning him the nickname "Ecuadorian Bukele".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mella |first=Carolina |date=2023-06-19 |title=Jan Topic, the millionaire businessman vying to emulate Bukele in Ecuador to the sound of 'Top Gun' |url=https://english.elpais.com/international/2023-06-19/jan-topic-the-millionaire-businessman-vying-to-emulate-bukele-in-ecuador-to-the-sound-of-top-gun.html |access-date=2023-08-02 |website=EL PAÍS English |language=es |archive-date=2 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230802142320/https://english.elpais.com/international/2023-06-19/jan-topic-the-millionaire-businessman-vying-to-emulate-bukele-in-ecuador-to-the-sound-of-top-gun.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=2023-06-27 |title=Jan Topic, el "Bukele ecuatoriano" que aspira a la presidencia |url=https://www.rfi.fr/es/m%C3%A1s-noticias/20230627-jan-topic-el-bukele-ecuatoriano-que-aspira-a-la-presidencia |access-date=2023-08-02 |website=RFI |archive-date=2 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230802142319/https://www.rfi.fr/es/m%C3%A1s-noticias/20230627-jan-topic-el-bukele-ecuatoriano-que-aspira-a-la-presidencia |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Jan Topic, el candidato que ofrece "mano dura" al estilo de Bukele |url=https://www.primicias.ec/noticias/elecciones-presidenciales-2023/jan-topic-bukele-candidato-perfil/ |access-date=2023-08-02 |website=Primicias |date=16 June 2023 |language=es-MX |archive-date=2 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230802142320/https://www.primicias.ec/noticias/elecciones-presidenciales-2023/jan-topic-bukele-candidato-perfil/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Although the other Ecuadorian candidates have also talked about dealing with crime with ruthless measures as a key priority, he and former Vice President [[Otto Sonnenholzner]] are the only candidates who have tried to imitate Bukele's image to varying degrees. Ecuador has seen a major increase in organized crime in the past two years.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Daniels |first=Joe |date=2023-05-26 |title=Drug wars threaten Ecuador's stability ahead of election |work=Financial Times |url=https://www.ft.com/content/17cd1481-f7f1-408b-bb65-84386964d343 |access-date=2023-08-02 |archive-date=2 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230802143035/https://www.ft.com/content/17cd1481-f7f1-408b-bb65-84386964d343 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Daniel Quintero]], the mayor of [[Medellín]] in Colombia, has also announced that he would be opening a prison in a similar style to the mega&shy;prisons run in El Salvador. Other Colombian politicians have also demanded similar security policies.<ref name="ulle">{{Cite web |last=Santaeulalia |first=Inés |date=2023-03-21 |title=Nayib Bukele replaces Uribe as inspiration for the right in Colombia |url=https://english.elpais.com/international/2023-03-21/nayib-bukele-replaces-uribe-as-inspiration-for-the-right-in-colombia.html |access-date=2023-06-14 |website=EL PAÍS English |language=en-us |archive-date=14 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230614080140/https://english.elpais.com/international/2023-03-21/nayib-bukele-replaces-uribe-as-inspiration-for-the-right-in-colombia.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Santaeulalia |first=Inés |date=2023-05-17 |title=The Colombian 'Bukeles' want power |url=https://english.elpais.com/international/2023-05-17/the-colombian-bukeles-want-power.html |access-date=2023-06-14 |website=EL PAÍS English |language=en-us |archive-date=14 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230614080138/https://english.elpais.com/international/2023-05-17/the-colombian-bukeles-want-power.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-03-17 |title=Al estilo de Nayib Bukele, alcalde Daniel Quintero anuncia nueva cárcel en Medellín |url=https://www.semana.com/confidenciales/articulo/al-estilo-de-nayib-bukele-alcalde-daniel-quintero-anuncia-nueva-carcel-en-medellin/202304/ |access-date=2023-06-13 |website=Semana.com Últimas Noticias de Colombia y el Mundo |language=es |archive-date=21 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230321185936/https://www.semana.com/confidenciales/articulo/al-estilo-de-nayib-bukele-alcalde-daniel-quintero-anuncia-nueva-carcel-en-medellin/202304/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On the other hand, Colombian President [[Gustavo Petro]] criticized the crackdown, drawing rebuke from Bukele.<ref>{{Cite web|first=Juan David|last=Rojas|date=March 23, 2023|title=On Security, Bukele and Petro Have a Problem in Common|url=https://americasquarterly.org/article/on-security-bukele-and-petro-have-a-problem-in-common/|access-date=2023-08-01|website=[[Americas Quarterly]]|language=en-US|archive-date=1 August 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230801104416/https://americasquarterly.org/article/on-security-bukele-and-petro-have-a-problem-in-common/|url-status=live}}</ref> The crackdown has been extremely popular in neighboring [[Honduras]] and [[Guatemala]], where some groups have even organized parades demanding similar policies. It has also been a point of debate and curiosity in Mexico<ref>{{Cite web|first=Christopher|last=Ramirez|title=The Bukelization of Mexico:The Influence of Nayib Bukele|url=https://latinamericanpost.com/42089-the-bukelization-of-mexico-the-influence-of-nayib-bukele|work=Latin American Post|date=16 September 2022|access-date=2 August 2023|archive-date=2 August 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230802104145/https://latinamericanpost.com/42089-the-bukelization-of-mexico-the-influence-of-nayib-bukele|url-status=live}}</ref> and in Chile, where crime rates have recently{{when|date=May 2026}} surged.<ref name="Delta"/> In addition it has been supported in [[Ecuador]], where a recent{{when|date=May 2026}} poll has showed that more than half of the population would support similar strategies<ref name=":0" /> and [[Colombia]], where the right wing has been demanding similar crackdowns.<ref name="ulle" /> Argentinian Security Minister [[Patricia Bullrich]] has expressed interest in using the "Bukele model" to tackle crime in Argentina, following an offer to cooperate from Bukele.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.batimes.com.ar/news/argentina/patricia-bullrich-wed-like-to-adapt-the-bukele-model.phtml | title=Patricia Bullrich: 'We'd like to adapt the Bukele model' to Argentina &#124; Buenos Aires Times | date=6 February 2024 }}</ref>

===Allegations of human rights abuses===
Human rights advocates have criticized the arrests as often arbitrary, based on a person's appearance or residence, and expressed concern that innocent people are being caught in the sweeps.<ref name="AP: extension" /> Bukele expressed hope that only 1% of arrests would be incorrect,<ref name="EF">{{cite news|date=12 April 2022|title=Bukele tiene un mensaje para Biden: 'Sí recibimos apoyo de EU para combatir pandillas... pero con Trump'|url=https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/mundo/2022/04/12/bukele-tiene-un-mensaje-para-biden-si-recibimos-apoyo-de-eu-para-combatir-pandillas-pero-con-trump/|work=El Financerio|language=es|access-date=28 May 2022|archive-date=4 July 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220704080843/https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/mundo/2022/04/12/bukele-tiene-un-mensaje-para-biden-si-recibimos-apoyo-de-eu-para-combatir-pandillas-pero-con-trump/|url-status=live}}</ref> and Salvadoran Defense Minister [[René Merino Monroy]] stated that arrested persons found to have no links to gangs would be freed, saying that "the people need to know that if they're not involved in anything bad, nothing bad will happen to them." [[Human Rights Watch]] criticized the government's policy as "first arrest, then tweet, and investigate later", referencing Salvadoran police's tweets depicting people's arrests.<ref name="BBC: success">{{cite news|date=26 April 2022|title=El Salvador anti-gang measures 'a success' as 17,000 held|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-61229116|publisher=[[BBC News]]|access-date=28 May 2022|archive-date=27 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220527083018/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-61229116|url-status=live}}</ref> Human rights groups have also expressed concern that the arrests have little to do with gang violence, suggesting Bukele will use them to consolidate power and target critics.<ref name="NYTimes"/>

Central American human rights advocacy and research group Cristosal reported 261 fatalities, abuse, denial of medical care, and torture in Salvadoran prisons in 2024 in connection with the gang crackdown.<ref>{{cite news|title=At least 261 people have died in El Salvador's prisons under anti-gang crackdown, rights group says|url=https://apnews.com/article/bukele-el-salvador-gang-crackdown-prison-deaths-9d14cbb1ea35175d75d007f6faade61f|publisher=[[Associated Press]]|language=en-US|url-status=live|date=July 10, 2024|access-date=April 1, 2025|archive-date=April 1, 2025|archive-url=https://archive.today/20250401182956/https://apnews.com/article/bukele-el-salvador-gang-crackdown-prison-deaths-9d14cbb1ea35175d75d007f6faade61f}}</ref> In May 2025, Cristosal lawyer [[Ruth Eleonora López|Ruth López]] was arrested and remains in detention as of June 2026. By July 2025, Cristosal was reporting 427 deaths in Salvadoran prisons. The organization had to evacuate its staff from El Salvador amid increasing threats of repression by the government.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Anti-Corruption Watchdog Flees El Salvador Amid Crackdown |url=https://www.democracynow.org/2025/7/18/headlines/anti_corruption_watchdog_flees_el_salvador_amid_crackdown |access-date=2025-07-20 |website=Democracy Now! |language=en}}</ref> 

